Use a set to remember the elements already seen; otherwise the code complexity increases.

Process each of the $n$ elements once: remove it from the front, and push it back only if it has not been seen before.

```cpp
void removeRedundant(queue<int> &q) {
    set<int> s;
    int n = q.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int temp = q.front();
        q.pop();
        if (s.find(temp) != s.end()) {
            continue;
        } else {
            s.insert(temp);
            q.push(temp);
        }
    }
}
```

