**Structure of the linked list.** Each node stores a coefficient, a power and the address of the next node.

```
[5 | 2] -> [4 | 1] -> [2 | 0] -> NULL      =>  5x^2 + 4x^1 + 2x^0
 coeff pow   address
```

**Code**

```c
void add(struct Node *poly1, struct Node *poly2, struct Node *poly)
{
    while (poly1 && poly2) {
        if (poly1->pow > poly2->pow) {
            poly->pow = poly1->pow;
            poly->coeff = poly1->coeff;
            poly1 = poly1->next;
        }
        else if (poly1->pow < poly2->pow) {
            poly->pow = poly2->pow;
            poly->coeff = poly2->coeff;
            poly2 = poly2->next;
        }
        else {
            poly->pow = poly1->pow;
            poly->coeff = poly1->coeff + poly2->coeff;
            poly1 = poly1->next;
            poly2 = poly2->next;
        }
        poly->next = (struct Node *)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));   // new node
        poly = poly->next;
        poly->next = NULL;
    }

    // copy the remaining terms of whichever polynomial is left
    while (poly1 || poly2) {
        if (poly1) {
            poly->pow = poly1->pow;
            poly->coeff = poly1->coeff;
            poly1 = poly1->next;
        }
        else {
            poly->pow = poly2->pow;
            poly->coeff = poly2->coeff;
            poly2 = poly2->next;
        }
        poly->next = (struct Node *)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));   // new node
        poly = poly->next;
        poly->next = NULL;
    }
}
```

```c
Node *solve(Node *first, Node *second) {
    // only one element in the first list
    if (first->next == NULL) {
        first->next = second;
        return first;
    }
    Node *curr1 = first;
    Node *next1 = curr1->next;
    Node *curr2 = second;
    Node *next2 = curr2->next;

    while (next1 != NULL && curr2 != NULL) {
        if (curr2->data >= curr1->data && curr2->data <= next1->data) {
            // insert curr2 between curr1 and next1
            curr1->next = curr2;
            next2 = curr2->next;
            curr2->next = next1;
            curr1 = curr2;
            curr2 = next2;
        }
        else {
            // move curr1 and next1 forward
            curr1 = next1;
            next1 = next1->next;
            if (next1 == NULL) {
                curr1->next = curr2;
                return first;
            }
        }
    }
    return first;
}

Node *sort(Node *first, Node *second) {
    if (first == NULL) return second;
    if (second == NULL) return first;
    if (first->data <= second->data) {
        return solve(first, second);
    } else {
        return solve(second, first);
    }
}
```

Union: put the elements of both lists into a set (duplicates are removed automatically), then build a list from the set. Intersection: for every element of the first list, check whether it is present in the second list.

```cpp
Node *getUnion(Node *head1, Node *head2)
{
    Node *ans = new Node(-1);        // dummy node
    Node *head = ans;
    unordered_set<int> st;
    while (head1 != NULL) {
        st.insert(head1->data);
        head1 = head1->next;
    }
    while (head2 != NULL) {
        st.insert(head2->data);
        head2 = head2->next;
    }
    for (auto it : st) {
        Node *t = new Node(it);
        ans->next = t;
        ans = ans->next;
    }
    head = head->next;               // skip the dummy node (-1)
    return head;
}

Node *getIntersection(Node *head1, Node *head2)
{
    Node *result = NULL;
    Node *t1 = head1;
    while (t1 != NULL) {
        if (isPresent(head2, t1->data))
            push(result, t1->data);
        t1 = t1->next;
    }
    return result;
}
```

**Time complexity**

- `getUnion`: $O(m + n)$ (average case, using hashing).
- `getIntersection`: $O(m \cdot n)$, i.e. $O(n^2)$ for two lists of $n$ elements, since each element of the first list is searched in the second list.

**Reading the diagram.** The box only moves characters between positions, so it is a fixed permutation. Number the input positions 0..7: f0 u1 n2 c3 t4 i5 o6 n7. The output is n c t n o u f i, so

- out[0] comes from in[2] ('n')
- out[1] comes from in[3] ('c')
- out[2] comes from in[4] ('t')
- out[3] comes from in[7] ('n')
- out[4] comes from in[6] ('o')
- out[5] comes from in[1] ('u')
- out[6] comes from in[0] ('f')
- out[7] comes from in[5] ('i')

#### (a) Data structure X

X is an array of 8 integers, `X[i]` = index of the input character that goes to output position `i`:

$$X = \{2,\,3,\,4,\,7,\,6,\,1,\,0,\,5\}$$

```c
#define N 8
int X[N] = {2, 3, 4, 7, 6, 1, 0, 5};      /* the "connector" structure */

void apply(const int X[], const char in[], char out[])
{
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        out[i] = in[X[i]];                /* output position i takes input X[i] */
    out[N] = '\0';
}
```

Check: `apply(X, "function", out)` gives "nctnoufi". The time is O(N) and the extra space is O(1) besides the output.

#### (b) Inverse structure Y = X^-1

If `out[i] = in[X[i]]`, then `in[X[i]] = out[i]`. So the inverse is defined by `Y[X[i]] = i`.

```c
void inverse(const int X[], int Y[])
{
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        Y[X[i]] = i;
}

void apply_inverse(const int Y[], const char out[], char in[])
{
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        in[i] = out[Y[i]];
    in[N] = '\0';
}
```

For the given X:

$$Y = \{6,\,5,\,0,\,1,\,2,\,7,\,4,\,3\}$$

Check: in[0] = out[6] = f, in[1] = out[5] = u, in[2] = out[0] = n, in[3] = out[1] = c, in[4] = out[2] = t, in[5] = out[7] = i, in[6] = out[4] = o, in[7] = out[3] = n. This gives "function". Time is O(N).

**Idea.** Find the middle node with the slow/fast pointer technique (slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps). Then link the new node after the middle node. If the list has an even number of nodes, the middle is taken as the first of the two central nodes.

**Node:**
```c
struct node { int data; struct node *prev, *next; };
```

**Algorithm InsertMiddle(head, x):**
1. Create a new node `p`; set `p->data = x`.
2. If `head == NULL`: set `p->prev = p->next = NULL`, `head = p`, and stop.
3. Set `slow = head`, `fast = head->next`.
4. While `fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL`: `slow = slow->next`, `fast = fast->next->next`.
5. Now `slow` is the middle node. Set `p->next = slow->next` and `p->prev = slow`.
6. If `slow->next != NULL`, set `slow->next->prev = p`.
7. Set `slow->next = p`.
8. Return `head`.

```c
struct node* insertMiddle(struct node *head, int x)
{
    struct node *p = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    p->data = x;
    if (head == NULL) { p->prev = p->next = NULL; return p; }

    struct node *slow = head, *fast = head->next;
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
    }
    p->next = slow->next;
    p->prev = slow;
    if (slow->next) slow->next->prev = p;
    slow->next = p;
    return head;
}
```

Time is O(n) and extra space is O(1). The order of steps 5-7 matters, because `slow->next` must not be overwritten before it is used.

**Idea.** The list is sorted, so equal values are adjacent. Walk through the list keeping a `prev` pointer. When `cur->data == cur->next->data`, `cur` is the first of the two equal nodes, so unlink and free it. Then stop, because only one duplicate pair exists.

**Algorithm:**
1. If the list is empty or has one node, return.
2. Set `prev = NULL`, `cur = head`.
3. While `cur->next != NULL`:
   - If `cur->data == cur->next->data`:
     - If `prev == NULL`, set `head = cur->next`. Otherwise set `prev->next = cur->next`.
     - Free `cur` and return `head`.
   - Else set `prev = cur`, `cur = cur->next`.
4. Return `head` (no duplicate found).

```c
struct node { int data; struct node *next; };

struct node* deleteFirstDuplicate(struct node *head)
{
    struct node *prev = NULL, *cur = head;
    while (cur && cur->next) {
        if (cur->data == cur->next->data) {
            if (prev == NULL) head = cur->next;   /* duplicate at the front */
            else prev->next = cur->next;
            free(cur);
            return head;
        }
        prev = cur;
        cur = cur->next;
    }
    return head;
}
```

Example: 1 -> 3 -> 5 -> 5 -> 8 becomes 1 -> 3 -> 5 -> 8. Time is O(n) and space is O(1).

**Idea.** Traverse the given list. Copy every alternate node (1st, 3rd, 5th, ...) into a new list, keeping a tail pointer so each insertion is O(1).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node { int data; struct node *next; };

struct node* newNode(int x)
{
    struct node *p = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    p->data = x; p->next = NULL;
    return p;
}

struct node* alternate(struct node *head)
{
    struct node *newHead = NULL, *tail = NULL;
    int take = 1;                         /* 1 = copy this node, 0 = skip it */
    for (struct node *cur = head; cur; cur = cur->next) {
        if (take) {
            struct node *p = newNode(cur->data);
            if (!newHead) newHead = tail = p;
            else { tail->next = p; tail = p; }
        }
        take = !take;
    }
    return newHead;
}
```

Example: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> 50 gives the new list 10 -> 30 -> 50. The original list is unchanged. Time is O(n).

#### Priority queue

A **priority queue** is an abstract data type in which every element has a priority. The element removed by `delete` is the one with the **highest priority**, not the oldest one. Elements of equal priority are served in FIFO order. It supports:

- `insert(item, priority)`
- `delete()`, which removes the highest-priority item
- `peek()` and `isEmpty()`

Uses: CPU/process scheduling, Dijkstra and Prim algorithms, Huffman coding, event simulation, and interrupt handling.

**Types:** an ascending priority queue removes the smallest key first. A descending priority queue removes the largest key first.

#### Linked-list implementation

Each node stores `data`, `priority` and `next`:
```c
struct pnode { int data; int pri; struct pnode *next; };
```

**Method 1: sorted linked list (most common).** The list is kept ordered by priority (highest priority at the head, smaller number = higher priority).

- `insert`: walk the list to find the correct position and link the node there. This costs O(n). For equal priorities, insert after the existing nodes with the same priority so FIFO order is kept.
- `delete`: remove the head node. This costs O(1).

```c
struct pnode* insert(struct pnode *head, int d, int pr)
{
    struct pnode *p = malloc(sizeof(struct pnode));
    p->data = d; p->pri = pr;
    if (head == NULL || pr < head->pri) {      /* new highest priority */
        p->next = head; return p;
    }
    struct pnode *cur = head;
    while (cur->next && cur->next->pri <= pr)
        cur = cur->next;
    p->next = cur->next;
    cur->next = p;
    return head;
}

struct pnode* delete_hp(struct pnode *head, int *d)   /* remove highest priority */
{
    if (head == NULL) { printf("Underflow\n"); return NULL; }
    struct pnode *t = head;
    *d = t->data;
    head = head->next;
    free(t);
    return head;
}
```

**Method 2: unsorted list.** `insert` adds at the front or rear in O(1). `delete` scans the whole list for the highest priority and removes it in O(n).

**Method 3: one queue per priority.** An array of linked-list queues, one for each priority level. `insert` is O(1). `delete` takes the first non-empty queue.

| Implementation | Insert | Delete |
|---|---|---|
| Sorted linked list | O(n) | O(1) |
| Unsorted linked list | O(1) | O(n) |
| Binary heap (for comparison) | O(log n) | O(log n) |

The linked list grows dynamically, so there is no overflow and no fixed size. It needs extra memory for the pointers.

**Problem.** Given an array with positive and negative integers, find the pair whose sum has the smallest absolute value.

**Method 1 (brute force), O(n^2).** Check all pairs and keep the pair with the minimum $|a[i]+a[j]|$.

**Method 2 (sort + two pointers), O(n log n).**
1. Sort the array.
2. Set `l = 0`, `r = n-1`.
3. Compute `sum = a[l] + a[r]`. If $|sum|$ is smaller than the best so far, record the pair.
4. If `sum < 0`, increase `l` (we need a bigger sum). If `sum > 0`, decrease `r`. If `sum == 0`, stop, because it cannot be better.
5. Repeat while `l < r`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int cmp(const void *a, const void *b) { return *(int*)a - *(int*)b; }

void closestToZero(int a[], int n)
{
    if (n < 2) { printf("Need at least two elements\n"); return; }
    qsort(a, n, sizeof(int), cmp);

    int l = 0, r = n - 1;
    int bestL = l, bestR = r, minAbs = abs(a[l] + a[r]);

    while (l < r) {
        int sum = a[l] + a[r];
        if (abs(sum) < minAbs) { minAbs = abs(sum); bestL = l; bestR = r; }
        if (sum < 0) l++;
        else if (sum > 0) r--;
        else break;
    }
    printf("Pair: %d and %d (sum = %d)\n", a[bestL], a[bestR], a[bestL] + a[bestR]);
}

int main(void)
{
    int a[] = {1, 60, -10, 70, -80, 85};
    closestToZero(a, 6);                  /* Pair: -80 and 85 (sum = 5) */
    return 0;
}
```

Dry run on {1, 60, -10, 70, -80, 85}: sorted = -80, -10, 1, 60, 70, 85.

- (-80, 85) gives 5. Sum is positive, so move `r` down.
- (-80, 70) gives -10. Sum is negative, so move `l` up.
- (-10, 70) gives 60, then (-10, 60) gives 50, then (-10, 1) gives -9, and so on. None is better than 5.

The answer is (-80, 85). Time is O(n log n) for the sort plus O(n) for the scan. Extra space is O(1).

**Given:** a singly linked list with only `data` and `next`. **Required:** a doubly linked list, so every node must also have a correct `prev` pointer.

```c
struct dnode { int data; struct dnode *prev, *next; };
```

**Algorithm (in place, O(n)):**
1. If the list is empty, return.
2. Set `prev_node = NULL`, `cur = head`.
3. While `cur != NULL`:
   - Set `cur->prev = prev_node`.
   - Set `prev_node = cur`.
   - Set `cur = cur->next`.
4. The head is unchanged, and its `prev` is NULL. Optionally, `prev_node` now points to the tail.

```c
void makeDoubly(struct dnode *head)
{
    struct dnode *prev = NULL;
    for (struct dnode *cur = head; cur != NULL; cur = cur->next) {
        cur->prev = prev;
        prev = cur;
    }
}
```

If the original nodes must stay untouched, create a new node for each element, copy its data, and link it to the tail of the new list. Set `new->prev = tail` and `tail->next = new`, then `tail = new`.

**Assumption.** The priority queue is a singly linked list kept sorted so that the highest-priority element is at the front (lowest number = highest priority). Deletion removes this element.

**Algorithm DeleteHP(front):**
1. If `front == NULL`, print "Queue underflow" and return.
2. Set `temp = front`.
3. Save `item = temp->data`.
4. Set `front = front->next`.
5. Free `temp`.
6. Return `item`.

```c
struct pnode { int data, pri; struct pnode *next; };

int deletePQ(struct pnode **front)
{
    if (*front == NULL) { printf("Underflow\n"); return -1; }
    struct pnode *temp = *front;
    int item = temp->data;
    *front = temp->next;
    free(temp);
    return item;
}
```

Time is O(1). If the list is not kept sorted, step 1 is replaced by a scan of the whole list that finds the node with the highest priority and its predecessor. That node is then unlinked with `prev->next = node->next`, which costs O(n).

**Idea.** Walk both lists together. Take one node from A, then one from B, and append copies to C, using a tail pointer. When one list is exhausted, append all remaining nodes of the other list.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node { int data; struct node *next; };

static struct node* newNode(int x)
{
    struct node *p = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    p->data = x; p->next = NULL;
    return p;
}

static void append(struct node **head, struct node **tail, int x)
{
    struct node *p = newNode(x);
    if (*head == NULL) *head = *tail = p;
    else { (*tail)->next = p; *tail = p; }
}

struct node* alternateMerge(struct node *A, struct node *B)
{
    struct node *C = NULL, *tail = NULL;

    while (A != NULL && B != NULL) {
        append(&C, &tail, A->data);     /* from A first */
        append(&C, &tail, B->data);     /* then from B  */
        A = A->next;
        B = B->next;
    }
    while (A != NULL) { append(&C, &tail, A->data); A = A->next; }  /* leftover of A */
    while (B != NULL) { append(&C, &tail, B->data); B = B->next; }  /* leftover of B */
    return C;
}
```

Example: A = 1 -> 3 -> 5 -> 7 and B = 2 -> 4 gives C = 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 7.

A and B are not modified. Time is O(m+n) and the new list uses O(m+n) space. (If the nodes of A and B may be reused, the same loop can relink the existing nodes instead of creating new ones.)

**Node:** `struct node { int data; struct node *prev, *next; };`

Insert the new node `p` **after** the node `q` (to insert before `q`, use the mirror image given in the last case below). The cases are:

**Case 1: list is empty (`head == NULL`).**
`p->prev = p->next = NULL; head = p;`

**Case 2: `q` is the last node (`q->next == NULL`).**
`p->next = NULL; p->prev = q; q->next = p;`

**Case 3: `q` is an intermediate or first node (`q->next != NULL`).**
```
p->next = q->next;
p->prev = q;
q->next->prev = p;
q->next = p;
```

```c
void insertAfter(struct node **head, struct node *q, struct node *p)
{
    if (*head == NULL) { p->prev = p->next = NULL; *head = p; return; }
    p->prev = q;
    p->next = q->next;
    if (q->next != NULL)          /* not the last node */
        q->next->prev = p;
    q->next = p;
}
```

**Insert before `q`** (the mirror image):
- If `q->prev == NULL`, `q` is the first node. Do `p->prev = NULL; p->next = q; q->prev = p; *head = p;`.
- Otherwise do `p->prev = q->prev; p->next = q; q->prev->next = p; q->prev = p;`.

The order of the pointer updates matters, so that no link is lost. Time is O(1).

