**Approach:** reverse a copy of the list (`head2` is a copy of `head1`), then compare it node by node with the original list.

```c
void reverse(struct Node **head2)
{
    struct Node *prev = NULL;
    struct Node *current = *head2;
    struct Node *next;
    while (current != NULL)
    {
        next = current->next;
        current->next = prev;
        prev = current;
        current = next;
    }
    *head2 = prev;
}
```

The three pointers used are `prev`, `current` and `next`.

**Compare**

```c
int compare(struct Node *h1, struct Node *h2)
{
    struct Node *t1 = h1, *t2 = h2;
    while (t1 && t2)
    {
        if (t1->data == t2->data) {
            t1 = t1->next;
            t2 = t2->next;
        }
        else
            return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

**Approach:** Dutch flag style partitioning. A single pass with a pointer `j` marking the end of the even part; every even element found is swapped to position `j`.

```c
void Partition(int A[], int n) {
    int j = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (A[i] % 2 == 0) {
            swap(A[i], A[j]);
            j++;
        }
    }
}
```

Time is $O(n)$ and only constant extra space is used.

Use the Dutch national flag algorithm with three pointers: `lo` (end of the cold region), `mid` (current element) and `hi` (start of the hot region).

- `C`: swap `nums[lo]` and `nums[mid]`, then `lo++`, `mid++`.
- `W`: `mid++`.
- `H`: swap `nums[mid]` and `nums[hi]`, then `hi--` (`mid` is not advanced).

```c
void Sort(char nums[], int n) {
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1, mid = 0;
    while (mid <= hi) {
        switch (nums[mid]) {
        case 'C':
            swap(nums[lo++], nums[mid++]);
            break;
        case 'W':
            mid++;
            break;
        case 'H':
            swap(nums[mid], nums[hi--]);
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

The algorithm makes one pass, so it runs in $O(N)$ time with constant extra space.

