**Approach:** Use one array `a[MAX]` shared by two stacks. Stack 1 grows from the left end (`top1` starts at -1). Stack 2 grows from the right end (`top2` starts at MAX). The stacks overflow only when the whole array is full (`top1 + 1 == top2`). A single `push(sno, x)` and a single `pop(sno)` serve both stacks, where `sno` is 1 or 2.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX 10

int a[MAX];
int top1 = -1, top2 = MAX;

void push(int sno, int x)
{
    if (top1 + 1 == top2) {                 /* array full */
        printf("Overflow\n");
        return;
    }
    if (sno == 1) a[++top1] = x;
    else          a[--top2] = x;
}

int pop(int sno)
{
    if (sno == 1) {
        if (top1 == -1)  { printf("Underflow: stack 1 empty\n"); return -1; }
        return a[top1--];
    } else {
        if (top2 == MAX) { printf("Underflow: stack 2 empty\n"); return -1; }
        return a[top2++];
    }
}

int main(void)
{
    int ch, sno, x;
    while (1) {
        printf("1.Push 2.Pop 3.Exit: ");
        scanf("%d", &ch);
        if (ch == 3) break;
        printf("Stack number (1 or 2): ");
        scanf("%d", &sno);
        if (sno != 1 && sno != 2) { printf("Invalid stack\n"); continue; }
        if (ch == 1) {
            printf("Value: ");
            scanf("%d", &x);
            push(sno, x);
        } else if (ch == 2) {
            x = pop(sno);
            if (x != -1) printf("Popped %d\n", x);
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

(An alternative is `struct Stack {int arr[MAX]; int top;}` with `push(struct Stack *s, int x)` and `pop(struct Stack *s)`. The same two functions then work for any stack passed to them.) Both `push` and `pop` run in $O(1)$.

**Data structure:** a stack of operands.

**Idea:** Scan the postfix expression left to right.

- If the symbol is an operand, push it.
- If it is an operator, pop the top two values (first pop = `op2`, second pop = `op1`), compute `op1 operator op2`, and push the result.
- At the end, the stack must contain exactly one value. That value is the result.

**Algorithm EvalPostfix(P)**

```
1. Create an empty stack S
2. for each symbol ch in P (left to right):
3.     if ch is a space: continue
4.     if ch is an operand (digit):          push(S, value of ch)
5.     else if ch is an operator (+ - * / ^):
6.         if S has fewer than 2 elements:
7.             print "Error: insufficient operands for operator ch"; exit
8.         op2 = pop(S); op1 = pop(S)
9.         if ch is '/' and op2 == 0:
10.            print "Error: division by zero"; exit
11.        push(S, op1 ch op2)
12.    else:
13.        print "Error: invalid symbol ch"; exit
14. if S has exactly 1 element: result = pop(S); print result
15. else if S is empty: print "Error: empty expression"
16. else: print "Error: too many operands (operators missing)"
```

**C implementation (single-digit operands):**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#include <math.h>
#define MAX 100

int stk[MAX], top = -1;

int evalPostfix(char *p, int *result)
{
    int i, a, b;
    top = -1;
    for (i = 0; p[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        char ch = p[i];
        if (ch == ' ') continue;
        if (isdigit(ch)) {
            if (top == MAX - 1) { printf("Error: stack overflow\n"); return 0; }
            stk[++top] = ch - '0';
        } else if (ch=='+'||ch=='-'||ch=='*'||ch=='/'||ch=='^') {
            if (top < 1) {
                printf("Error: insufficient operands for '%c'\n", ch);
                return 0;
            }
            b = stk[top--];
            a = stk[top--];
            switch (ch) {
                case '+': stk[++top] = a + b; break;
                case '-': stk[++top] = a - b; break;
                case '*': stk[++top] = a * b; break;
                case '/':
                    if (b == 0) { printf("Error: division by zero\n"); return 0; }
                    stk[++top] = a / b; break;
                case '^': stk[++top] = (int)pow(a, b); break;
            }
        } else {
            printf("Error: invalid symbol '%c'\n", ch);
            return 0;
        }
    }
    if (top == 0)  { *result = stk[top]; return 1; }
    if (top == -1) printf("Error: empty expression\n");
    else           printf("Error: too many operands (missing operator)\n");
    return 0;
}
```

**Error conditions detected**

| Error | How detected |
|---|---|
| Too few operands, e.g. `5 +` or `2 3 + *` | operator found but stack has fewer than 2 elements |
| Too many operands, e.g. `2 3 4 +` | after the scan, stack has more than 1 element |
| Invalid symbol, e.g. `2 3 $` | symbol is neither operand nor operator |
| Division by zero, e.g. `4 0 /` | divisor `op2 == 0` |
| Empty expression | stack empty at the end |

**Example:** Evaluate `5 3 2 * + 4 -`

| Symbol | Action | Stack (bottom to top) |
|---|---|---|
| 5 | push | 5 |
| 3 | push | 5 3 |
| 2 | push | 5 3 2 |
| * | 3*2=6 | 5 6 |
| + | 5+6=11 | 11 |
| 4 | push | 11 4 |
| - | 11-4=7 | 7 |

Result = **7**.

**Complexity:** $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space for an expression of $n$ symbols. For multi-digit operands, read tokens separated by spaces and convert each with `atoi` or a number-building loop.

