**Stack overflow** occurs when a push is attempted on a full stack. **Stack underflow** occurs when a pop is attempted on an empty stack.

#### (i) Stack using an array of size MAX (`top` initially = -1)

| Condition | Test | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Overflow (stack full) | `top == MAX - 1` | no free cell is left, so push is not possible |
| Underflow (stack empty) | `top == -1` | no element is present, so pop/peek is not possible |

```c
void push(int x){
    if (top == MAX - 1) { printf("Stack Overflow"); return; }
    a[++top] = x;
}
int pop(){
    if (top == -1) { printf("Stack Underflow"); return -1; }
    return a[top--];
}
```

The array has a fixed size, so overflow can really happen. It is a limitation of the static array.

#### (ii) Stack using a linked list (`top` points to the head node, initially NULL)

| Condition | Test | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Overflow (stack full) | `malloc` returns `NULL` (`newnode == NULL`) | the heap has no memory left. There is no fixed limit on the size otherwise. |
| Underflow (stack empty) | `top == NULL` | the list has no node, so pop/peek is not possible |

```c
void push(int x){
    struct node *t = (struct node*)malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    if (t == NULL) { printf("Stack Overflow (no memory)"); return; }
    t->data = x; t->next = top; top = t;
}
int pop(){
    if (top == NULL) { printf("Stack Underflow"); return -1; }
    struct node *t = top; int x = t->data;
    top = top->next; free(t);
    return x;
}
```

**Summary:** with an array, overflow is checked by `top == MAX-1`. With a linked list, a stack never fills up logically and overflow happens only when memory allocation fails. Underflow is checked by `top == -1` for the array and `top == NULL` for the linked list.

