The **inorder predecessor** and **inorder successor** of a node $X$ are the nodes that appear immediately before and immediately after $X$ in the in-order traversal.

Both are found in one function. Search for $X$ from the root: whenever we go left, the current node is a candidate successor; whenever we go right, it is a candidate predecessor. Then, if $X$ has a left subtree, the predecessor is its rightmost node; if $X$ has a right subtree, the successor is its leftmost node.

```cpp
pair<int, int> PredecessorSuccessor(Node *root, int X) {
    Node *temp = root;
    int pred = -1, succ = -1;
    while (temp->data != X) {
        if (temp->data > X) {
            succ = temp->data;
            temp = temp->left;
        }
        else {
            pred = temp->data;
            temp = temp->right;
        }
    }

    // predecessor
    Node *leftTree = temp->left;
    while (leftTree != NULL) {
        pred = leftTree->data;
        leftTree = leftTree->right;
    }

    // successor
    Node *rightTree = temp->right;
    while (rightTree != NULL) {
        succ = rightTree->data;
        rightTree = rightTree->left;
    }
    return {pred, succ};
}
```

