#### Min-heap property

In a min-heap, for every node $i$ other than the root:

$$A[\text{Parent}(i)] \le A[i]$$

- The smallest element is at the root.
- The subtree of a node contains no value smaller than the value at the node itself.

#### Max-heap property

In a max-heap, for every node $i$ other than the root:

$$A[\text{Parent}(i)] \ge A[i]$$

- The largest element is stored at the root.
- The subtree rooted at a node contains no value larger than the value at the node itself.

A heap is represented using an **array** (as a complete binary tree).

#### Algorithm

Use Max-Heapify on every non-leaf node, from the last one up to the root.

```cpp
void heapify(int arr[], int size, int pi) {
    int lci = 2 * pi + 1;
    int rci = 2 * pi + 2;
    int maxi = pi;
    if (lci < size && arr[maxi] < arr[lci])
        maxi = lci;
    if (rci < size && arr[maxi] < arr[rci])
        maxi = rci;
    if (maxi != pi) {
        swap(arr[maxi], arr[pi]);
        heapify(arr, size, maxi);
    }
}

void buildMaxHeap(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        heapify(arr, n, i);
}
```

**Implementation:** A priority queue is implemented efficiently with a **binary min-heap**, stored in an array. The heap is a complete binary tree, where each node's key is $\le$ the keys of its children, so the entry with the smallest key is always at index 0. For the node at index $i$: parent $=\lfloor (i-1)/2\rfloor$, left child $=2i+1$, right child $=2i+2$.

- `insert(k, v)`: place the new entry at the end and **up-heap** (bubble up) while its key is smaller than its parent's key. Time $O(\log n)$.
- `removeMin()`: save the root, move the last entry to the root, and **down-heap** (bubble down) by swapping with the smaller child until the heap property holds. Time $O(\log n)$.
- `min()`, `size()`, `isEmpty()`: $O(1)$.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    int key;
    int value;
} Entry;

typedef struct {
    Entry *arr;
    int size;
    int capacity;
} PriorityQueue;

PriorityQueue *createPQ(int capacity)
{
    PriorityQueue *pq = (PriorityQueue *)malloc(sizeof(PriorityQueue));
    pq->arr = (Entry *)malloc(capacity * sizeof(Entry));
    pq->size = 0;
    pq->capacity = capacity;
    return pq;
}

int isEmpty(PriorityQueue *pq) { return pq->size == 0; }
int size(PriorityQueue *pq)    { return pq->size; }

static void swap(Entry *a, Entry *b) { Entry t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }

void insert(PriorityQueue *pq, int k, int v)
{
    if (pq->size == pq->capacity) {              /* grow the array when full */
        pq->capacity *= 2;
        pq->arr = (Entry *)realloc(pq->arr, pq->capacity * sizeof(Entry));
    }
    int i = pq->size++;
    pq->arr[i].key = k;
    pq->arr[i].value = v;
    while (i > 0 && pq->arr[(i - 1) / 2].key > pq->arr[i].key) {   /* up-heap */
        swap(&pq->arr[i], &pq->arr[(i - 1) / 2]);
        i = (i - 1) / 2;
    }
}

Entry min(PriorityQueue *pq)                     /* peek at the smallest entry */
{
    if (isEmpty(pq)) { printf("Priority queue is empty\n"); exit(1); }
    return pq->arr[0];
}

Entry removeMin(PriorityQueue *pq)
{
    if (isEmpty(pq)) { printf("Priority queue is empty\n"); exit(1); }
    Entry result = pq->arr[0];
    pq->arr[0] = pq->arr[--pq->size];            /* move last entry to the root */
    int i = 0;
    while (1) {                                  /* down-heap */
        int l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2, smallest = i;
        if (l < pq->size && pq->arr[l].key < pq->arr[smallest].key) smallest = l;
        if (r < pq->size && pq->arr[r].key < pq->arr[smallest].key) smallest = r;
        if (smallest == i) break;
        swap(&pq->arr[i], &pq->arr[smallest]);
        i = smallest;
    }
    return result;
}

int main(void)
{
    PriorityQueue *pq = createPQ(4);
    insert(pq, 5, 50);
    insert(pq, 2, 20);
    insert(pq, 8, 80);
    insert(pq, 1, 10);
    while (!isEmpty(pq)) {
        Entry e = removeMin(pq);
        printf("(%d, %d)\n", e.key, e.value);    /* (1,10) (2,20) (5,50) (8,80) */
    }
    free(pq->arr);
    free(pq);
    return 0;
}
```

**Complexity:**

| Operation | Time |
|---|---|
| insert(k, v) | $O(\log n)$ |
| removeMin() | $O(\log n)$ |
| min(), size(), isEmpty() | $O(1)$ |

Space is $O(n)$. (An unsorted or sorted list would make one of insert/removeMin cost $O(n)$, so the heap is better.)

A binary tree is a (max) heap if:

- it is a complete binary tree, and
- every node's value is greater than or equal to the values of its children.

```cpp
int countNodes(Node* root) {
    if (root == NULL)
        return 0;
    return 1 + countNodes(root->left) + countNodes(root->right);
}

// checks that the tree is complete; n = total number of nodes
bool isComplete(Node* root, int index, int n) {
    if (root == NULL)
        return true;
    if (index >= n)
        return false;
    return isComplete(root->left, 2 * index + 1, n) &&
           isComplete(root->right, 2 * index + 2, n);
}

// checks that every node is >= its children
bool isMaxOrdered(Node* root) {
    if (root->left == NULL && root->right == NULL)
        return true;
    if (root->right == NULL)
        return root->key >= root->left->key && isMaxOrdered(root->left);
    if (root->key >= root->left->key && root->key >= root->right->key)
        return isMaxOrdered(root->left) && isMaxOrdered(root->right);
    return false;
}

bool isHeap(Node* root) {
    if (root == NULL)
        return true;
    return isComplete(root, 0, countNodes(root)) && isMaxOrdered(root);
}
```
